  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nAs the area continues to relish the dry weather overall due to \nlight wind speeds and RH values not plummeting during the \nafternoons, fire weather concerns are mitigated for now. High \npressure is still forecast to dominate into the new work week \nuntil the next chances of rainfall come into play. \n\nThere is a subtle trough due for Monday morning which brings \nslight chances for rain showers across mainly the southeast \nOhio and northeast Kentucky areas, however this would be very \nlimited rainfall due to all the dry air in place and high\npressure. Not expecting any excessive rainfall or heavy shower \nactivity. Anything we do get would be beneficial rainfall, but \nwe are not expecting much in the way of a lot of activity until\nmid to late week. \n\nModels are in very large disagreement on timing and placement \nof several disturbances and systems, therefore confidence is \nlow on when and where the most rainfall will be and there is a \nlot of uncertainty at this time. If the medium rang model \nsolutions hold true then we should see areawide chances of \nrainfall mid to late week and then cutting off by Saturday. \n\nHowever, with the lack of confidence displayed in the medium \nrang models the chances are largely defined with the blend of \nmodels which has us in likely PoPs on Wednesday and going into \nThursday. Then chances taper down some on Friday and we are \nslated to get back to dry weather by Saturday albeit a \nrelatively short dry period as an active weather pattern takes \nhold. \n\nThe main them is that this could all change due to the model \ninconsistency, therefore the National Blend of Models is \nheavily weighted on this forecast for the aforementioned time \nrange. Progressively more active weather will likely come to \nfruition by mid week and continue for the most part through the \nrest of the forecast period and beyond.",
